Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.90,0:00:03.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Shine a laser on a wall, and the spot sparkles
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.69,0:00:07.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}with grainy speckle. An LED's spot is smooth.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.49,0:00:09.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Through two slits, the laser paints crisp fringes.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.81,0:00:14.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The LED paints none, unless it first shines through a tiny pinhole.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.95,0:00:19.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}What does a laser have, that an LED doesn't? In a word: coherence.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.99,0:00:23.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At any point, a light wave is an arrow, a complex number.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.84,0:00:26.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its length is the amplitude, its angle the phase.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.88,0:00:29.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}It spins hundreds of trillions of times a second,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.68,0:00:31.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}too fast for any camera,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.14,0:00:34.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}which records only its squared length: the intensity.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.71,0:00:38.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Where two waves meet, their arrows add. They spin together,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.24,0:00:41.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so only the angle between them matters: Δφ.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.15,0:00:43.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Squaring the sum gives both intensities,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.74,0:00:47.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}plus a cross term that depends on Δφ: interference.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.51,0:00:50.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Move across a screen, and Δφ grows steadily,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.10,0:00:52.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the cross term paints fringes.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.71,0:00:54.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now let one wave's phase jitter randomly,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.02,0:00:58.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}faster than the camera can follow. The fringes dance.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.55,0:01:02.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}If averaging removes the cross term, the intensities add.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.97,0:01:04.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The visibility measures what's left:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.52,0:01:07.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}one for perfect fringes, zero for none.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.51,0:01:11.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Why would phases wander? One reason is colour.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.23,0:01:15.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Split a beam, and delay one copy by a path difference, ΔL.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.47,0:01:18.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}With a single colour, the fringes go on forever.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.46,0:01:20.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}But each colour has its own spacing.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.64,0:01:24.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At zero delay they agree; further out, they drift apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.32,0:01:29.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Add a whole spectrum, and fringes survive only near zero delay.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:29.77,0:01:33.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That window is the coherence length, roughly λ²/Δλ.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.57,0:01:36.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In fact, the envelope is the Fourier transform of the spectrum:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.96,0:01:39.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}narrow spectrum, long coherence.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.84,0:01:42.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A good laser: centimetres to kilometres. An LED:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.02,0:01:47.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}about ten microns. Sunlight: about one micron.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.11,0:01:49.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Colour isn't the whole story.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.67,0:01:52.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A laser behaves like a single point of light;
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.57,0:01:56.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}an LED is a crowd of independent points.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.19,0:01:59.67,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Move one point off centre. Its light reaches the slits tilted,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.71,0:02:02.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the tilt shifts its fringes sideways.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.70,0:02:04.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Different points have unrelated phases,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.55,0:02:09.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so their intensities add. The camera sees a sum of shifted copies.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.23,0:02:12.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Watch as the source grows: the copies spread out and fill
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.66,0:02:16.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}in each other's dark bands, until the sum is flat grey.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.93,0:02:20.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Fringes vanish once the source's angular size
Dialogue: 0,0:02:20.49,0:02:22.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}reaches about λ over the slit separation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.94,0:02:25.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A tiny pinhole brings them back. So does distance:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.63,0:02:28.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a giant star, far away, looks tiny.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.93,0:02:32.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How visibility fades as the slits move apart is the Fourier
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.61,0:02:36.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}transform of the source's shape: the van Cittert–Zernike theorem.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.76,0:02:39.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In 1920, Michelson spread two mirrors
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.45,0:02:41.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}apart until a star's fringes vanished,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.85,0:02:44.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and measured the size of Betelgeuse.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.09,0:02:49.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Coherence has a dark side. A wall is rough on the scale of a wavelength.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.66,0:02:53.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At each pixel, light arrives from every bump with a random phase,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.31,0:02:56.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the arrows add up like a random walk.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.32,0:02:58.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Sometimes they cancel, sometimes they line up:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:58.60,0:03:01.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}dark and bright grains called speckle.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.34,0:03:05.71,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its contrast, the standard deviation over the mean, is exactly one.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.78,0:03:09.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Each grain is about λ/NA across: the numerical aperture,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.97,0:03:13.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}NA, is the sine of the widest angle collected.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.58,0:03:17.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Average N independent patterns, and the contrast falls as 1/√N.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.00,0:03:21.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}An LED's many colours and points do just that.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:22.60,0:03:25.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How do we compute with partially coherent light? Record,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:25.59,0:03:29.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}for every pair of points, how well their fields keep in step.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:29.46,0:03:33.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's the mutual intensity, J: the average of the field at x₁
Dialogue: 0,0:03:33.99,0:03:39.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}times the conjugate field at x₂. Its diagonal is the intensity.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:39.16,0:03:41.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Off the diagonal, it says how strongly two
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.64,0:03:44.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}points would interfere through a double slit.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.11,0:03:45.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Swapping the points conjugates J,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.00,0:03:49.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so it's a Hermitian matrix, and it can be diagonalized.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:49.85,0:03:51.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its eigenvectors are coherent modes,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:51.90,0:03:56.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}each perfectly coherent, and its eigenvalues are their powers.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.44,0:03:59.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So partially coherent light is a sum of independent
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.43,0:04:02.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}coherent fields, whose intensities add.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.12,0:04:07.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A laser needs one mode. The bigger the source, the more modes it takes.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:08.20,0:04:10.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}On a computer, that gives three recipes.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.34,0:04:13.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Coherent light: propagate the field, then square it.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:13.89,0:04:17.31,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Incoherent light: add intensities, often with a convolution.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.35,0:04:19.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Partially coherent light:
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.30,0:04:22.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}propagate a few modes, and add their intensities.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.50,0:04:24.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Here's a diffractive element lit by a laser.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.76,0:04:28.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its far-field pattern is crisp, but speckled.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.22,0:04:32.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}With an LED, each source point tilts the light, shifting the pattern,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.13,0:04:36.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and each colour stretches it. The sum is blurred, but smooth.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.20,0:04:39.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At large angles, this simple blur stops being exact,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.51,0:04:42.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}but a few coherent modes are often enough.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.06,0:04:45.71,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Fields always add; the exposure determines
Dialogue: 0,0:04:45.75,0:04:47.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}which interference terms survive.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.54,0:04:51.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}When the averaged cross term vanishes, intensities add.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.55,0:04:55.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}And partially coherent light is a sum of coherent patterns.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.21,0:04:58.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Next time: coherence lets us do what a camera can't,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.37,0:05:00.21,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and record the phase of light.
